.TH FD 4 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Device Drivers Manual"
.SH NAME
fd, stdin, stdout, stderr \- file descriptor pseudo-devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /dev/fd/\fIn\fP
.br
.B /dev/stdin
.br
.B /dev/stdout
.br
.B /dev/stderr
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B /dev/fd
directory provides a mechanism to access open file descriptors by number.
Opening
.BI /dev/fd/ n
is equivalent to duplicating file descriptor
.I n
using
.BR dup (2).
.PP
The following symbolic links provide convenient access to the standard
file descriptors:
.TP
.B /dev/stdin
Symbolic link to
.BR /dev/fd/0 .
.TP
.B /dev/stdout
Symbolic link to
.BR /dev/fd/1 .
.TP
.B /dev/stderr
Symbolic link to
.BR /dev/fd/2 .
.SH NOTES
This is the native Substrate interface for file descriptor access.
Linux processes use
.B /proc/self/fd
instead, which is provided via the Linux personality's procfs emulation.
.PP
The
.B /dev/fd
mechanism is compatible with BSD and POSIX systems.
.SH EXAMPLE
Redirect a filter's output to the original standard error:
.PP
.nf
    filter 2>/dev/fd/4 4>&2
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/fd/
Directory of open file descriptors.
.TP
.B /dev/stdin
Symlink to /dev/fd/0.
.TP
.B /dev/stdout
Symlink to /dev/fd/1.
.TP
.B /dev/stderr
Symlink to /dev/fd/2.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dup (2),
.BR open (2),
.BR intro (4)
